title: Cloud Custodian policies, hands-on
guide: howto/cloud-custodian.html
promise: Learn the policy language and the ways it fails quietly, on a pretend Amazon Web Services (AWS) account that runs on your own computer.

== Who it is for and what you need
section: How to use this guide
Cloud Custodian finds cloud resources that break your rules. The guide suits intermediate readers who have used AWS or another cloud and can read YAML, a plain-text format for structured data.

Allow about two hours; you need no AWS account.

== How the guide was checked
section: How to use this guide
A script ran every code block in order, in zsh on macOS, with Cloud Custodian 0.9.53. Outputs shown are from that run.

Step 8's deployed function never ran, and nothing ran against Azure, Google Cloud or a real AWS account.

== How to follow the guide
section: How to use this guide
Read in order. Paste each BASH block into your terminal whole, EOF line included: cat > file <<'EOF' saves the lines down to EOF as that file. Unlabelled blocks show what should print. Policy excerpts are parts of such files.

== Set up: install Cloud Custodian and moto
section: 1. Install Cloud Custodian and moto
Cloud Custodian 0.9.53 needs Python 3.10.2 or newer; check python3 --version. If yours is older, install a newer one and use its name, such as python3.12, on line 2. These lines also install moto, an open-source AWS emulator. Expect 0.9.53.
```bash
mkdir -p ~/c7n-lab && cd ~/c7n-lab
python3 -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate
pip install --quiet 'c7n==0.9.53' 'botocore==1.43.124' 'moto[server]==5.2.5'
custodian version
```

== Set up: start the lab, then point Custodian at it
section: 3. Point Custodian at the lab
Step 2's block writes lab.py and starts the lab, a pretend AWS account: paste it whole; leave it running. Expect Lab running at http://localhost:8116. These lines, in a second terminal, point Custodian at it. Rerun them in any new terminal.
```bash
cd ~/c7n-lab && . .venv/bin/activate
unset AWS_PROFILE AWS_SESSION_TOKEN
export AWS_ENDPOINT_URL=http://localhost:8116 AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=us-east-1
export AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=lab AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=lab
```

== What Cloud Custodian is and where you meet it
section: Cloud Custodian for security work
Much of cloud security work is finding the same few mistakes again and again. Cloud Custodian, an open-source rules engine for cloud accounts, finds them and can fix them, in a single run, on a timer, or on each change.

== A policy has up to four parts
section: The four parts of a policy
- The resource part names what to list.
- A resource must pass every filter, unless an or block says otherwise.
- Actions run on what is left.
- With no mode, a policy is pull: it runs once, now, from your machine.

== A broken policy and a clean account look the same
section: Why a broken policy and a clean account look the same
A filter on a missing or wrongly typed field usually answers false, with no error. So a broken policy and a clean account print the same count:0, zero matches. Before believing a policy, run it where something should match.

== Step 3: a policy with no filters matches everything
section: 3. Point Custodian at the lab
The rest of step 3's block runs buckets.yml, a policy with no filters: count:4, all four S3 (Simple Storage Service) buckets. resources.json, under the -s output directory, shows their fields.
```bash
cat > buckets.yml <<'EOF'
policies:
  - name: all-buckets
    resource: aws.s3
EOF
custodian run --cache-period 0 -s out buckets.yml
custodian report -s out buckets.yml --format simple
ls -1 out/all-buckets
```

== Step 4: a key the resource does not have
section: 4. Silent failure: a key the resource does not have
In step 4's versioning.yml, this value filter finds only lab-build-artifacts. Two buckets never had versioning, so Versioning.Status is missing, and every comparison with a missing key is false except ne and not-in. The value absent matches a missing key.
```bash
  - name: status-is-suspended
    resource: aws.s3
    filters:
      - type: value
        key: Versioning.Status
        value: Suspended
```

== Step 5: custodian validate accepts a typo
section: 5. Silent failure: custodian validate accepts a policy that can never match
Step 5's sg.yml looks twice for security groups, AWS's virtual firewalls, open to the whole internet; this copy misspells IpPermissions. custodian validate, which checks only the schema, accepts it. It finds nothing; the correct copy finds debug-ssh and web-public.
```bash
  - name: world-open-typo
    resource: aws.security-group
    filters:
      - type: value
        key: "IpPermission[].IpRanges[].CidrIp"
        op: contains
        value: 0.0.0.0/0
```

== Step 6: one Region unless you ask for more
section: 6. Silent failure: one Region unless you ask for more
A run covers one Region: the --region flag, else AWS_DEFAULT_REGION, else your config file's. Here -p picks world-open, and --region all visits the lab's 34 Regions. It also finds legacy-rdp, with RDP (Remote Desktop Protocol) open, in eu-west-1.
```bash
custodian run --cache-period 0 -s out-all --region all -p world-open sg.yml 2> all.log
grep -c policy:world-open all.log
grep -v count:0 all.log
```

== Step 7: --dryrun skips the actions and nothing else
section: 7. Silent failure: --dryrun skips the actions and nothing else
These lines end step 7's ssh-fix.yml, which removes rules that let the whole internet reach SSH (Secure Shell) on port 22. With --dryrun it finds debug-ssh, skips actions, and still writes output.
```bash
    filters:
      - type: ingress
        Ports: [22]
        Cidr:
          value: 0.0.0.0/0
    actions:
      - type: remove-permissions
        ingress: matched
```

== Step 7: a cached answer is not a fresh one
section: 7. Silent failure: --dryrun skips the actions and nothing else
Step 7's second block first runs ssh-fix.yml for real, removing debug-ssh's rule. Then world-open still counts two open groups, in time:0.00, since Custodian reuses what it listed for 15 minutes by default. With --cache-period 0, it counts one.
```bash
custodian run -s out -p world-open sg.yml
custodian run --cache-period 0 -s out -p world-open sg.yml
```

== Step 8: deployed into the account
section: 8. Modes: pull, or deployed into the account
Step 8's ssh-realtime.yml adds this mode to step 7's policy. Run without --dryrun, it creates an AWS Lambda function, run when CloudTrail records a call adding an inbound rule. The deploy checks no existing group: keep a scheduled pull run.
```bash
  - name: ssh-open-realtime
    resource: aws.security-group
    mode:
      type: cloudtrail
      role: arn:aws:iam::{account_id}:role/custodian-lambda
      events:
        - source: ec2.amazonaws.com
          event: AuthorizeSecurityGroupIngress
          ids: requestParameters.groupId
```

== Exercise: every security group open to the internet
section: Hands-on exercises
Find every security group with an inbound rule open to the whole internet, on any port, in every Region. Write it in ~/c7n-lab with the lab running and step 3's environment. The guide hides each answer under "Show the answer".

== Answer: test IPv4 and IPv6, in every Region
section: Hands-on exercises
Cidr tests only IPv4, so world-open.yml adds CidrV6 in an or block for ipv6-ssh's ::/0, the whole IPv6 internet. With --region all, expect web-public, ipv6-ssh, legacy-rdp; step 7 fixed debug-ssh.
```bash
policies:
  - name: sg-world-open
    resource: aws.security-group
    filters:
      - or:
          - type: ingress
            Cidr:
              value: 0.0.0.0/0
          - type: ingress
            CidrV6:
              value: "::/0"
```

== Exercise: access keys unused for 90 days
section: Hands-on exercises
List every IAM (Identity and Access Management) user with an active access key, a key pair programs sign requests with, unused for 90 days. Count a never-used key from its creation. Use the credential filter, which reads IAM's credential report.

== Answer: a never-used key needs its own policy
section: Hands-on exercises
An age test on last_used_date finds bob, but a never-used key has no date. stale-keys.yml's second policy, ending as shown, catches dave's key; last_rotated is when a key was created or changed. erin, with 0 keys matched, is no finding.
```bash
      - type: credential
        key: access_keys.last_used_date
        value: absent
      - type: credential
        key: access_keys.last_rotated
        value_type: age
        op: gt
        value: 90
```

== Common mistakes
section: Common mistakes
- Equality and ordering comparisons are false against a missing key; an empty {} counts as present.
- With --region all, S3 policies report each bucket once per Region.
- Without matched: true, the remove-keys action removes every key a matched user has.
